--- body ---
## Context

erg's one job is custody of tickets; losing or corrupting one is the worst,
irreversible failure — and nothing currently guards the data-safety
invariants as a suite. The mutating commands (`new`, `close`, `log`, `tag`,
`untag`, `rm`, `archive`, `migrate`, `update`) each rewrite or move files,
and each does its own `os.Rename`/`WriteFile`/`.tmp` dance — there is no
single obviously-shared atomic-write path. Step one is to confirm whether
mutations funnel through one audited helper; if they don't, that is a finding
(and possibly a precursor fix).

The realistic data-loss vector is not a red-teamer — it is a **user error or
an eager creative agent**: a fat-fingered `DIR`/ID, or an agent computing a
target outside `tickets/`. The cheapest rail against that is to **confine
erg's writes to the resolved store** (below). It is a fail-safe, not a
security boundary — it "can be removed" and a determined caller bypasses it —
but it stops the common mistake before it lands.

## The guards (each falsifiable, with a negative control)

- **Atomic replace** — every mutation writes a temp then renames over the
  target; a concurrent reader never sees a partial file. Test: no command
  truncates-in-place.
- **Validate-before-replace** — a mutation that would yield an invalid
  `%erg` file refuses and leaves the original untouched (never write garbage
  over a good ticket).
- **Lossless round-trip** — parse→write preserves the append-only log and
  the body verbatim; `close`/`tag`/`untag`/`rm --force` never drop or reorder
  log/body content (except the documented interior-blank normalisation).
  Golden before/after differs only in the intended line.
- **No-clobber** — `new`/`next-id` never overwrite an existing ID (`O_EXCL`);
  a collision errors rather than replacing.
- **Crash/interrupt safety** — a killed `erg` mid-mutation leaves either the
  complete old file or the complete new one, never a zero-length/truncated
  file. Test via a wrapper that interrupts around the rename.
- **DAG guard (regression of 0142)** — `rm` still refuses to orphan
  dependents without `--force`.
- **Write-confinement (fail-safe rail + behaviour addition)** — before any
  write/rename, the absolute target must resolve *inside* the store root, else
  refuse. Catches user errors and overeager agents writing outside `tickets/`.
  Not a security boundary (the adversarial version of this boundary is 0151's
  path-traversal); here it is mistake-prevention. Cross-ref 0146 stateless
  (read commands write nothing outside the store).

## Constraints (suite obeys the contract)

Shell + Go toolchain, offline, no new deps, deterministic. Each guard must
fail if its safety property is removed.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_datasafety.sh` (new) + `Makefile` `TEST_SUITES += datasafety`;
  Go unit tests for the write/rename helper if one is introduced.
- Touch points: `new.go`, `close.go`, `log.go`, `tag.go`, `rm.go`,
  `archive.go`, `migrate.go`, `update.go`.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Falsifiable guard per property (atomic, validate-before-replace,
      lossless round-trip, no-clobber, crash-safety, DAG), each with a
      negative control.
- [x] Write-confinement enforced at runtime (mutations refuse an out-of-store
      target) and guarded with a negative control.
- [x] Finding recorded on whether mutations share one atomic-write path; if
      not, a follow-up to consolidate is filed. (They did not — only update.go
      used temp+rename; consolidated into writeTicketAtomic/atomicWriteFile in
      this ticket, so no follow-up is needed.)
- [x] Wired into `make test`; `make test`/`make validate` green.

## Notes

- This is the **never-lose-data** half of the dreamlist `audit security`
  line; the **never-leak-keys / red-team** half is 0151.
- Pairs with the never-lose-data invariant now in AGENTS.md (the one job).
